<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 78 0 R
/T (Esports tournaments generate a large amount of structured data: who is playing, what map they played on, how many kills they secured and where they finish in the table. Organisers usually track this in spreadsheets, which are easy to break and hard to validate. A small object-oriented program is a good way to model the same domain, because every real-world thing \(a player, a match, a tournament\) maps neatly to a class.)
/E (Esports tournaments generate a large amount of structured data: who is playing, what map they played on, how many kills they secured and where they finish in the table. Organisers usually track this in spreadsheets, which are easy to break and hard to validate. A small object-oriented program is a good way to model the same domain, because every real-world thing \(a player, a match, a tournament\) maps neatly to a class.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 78 0 R
/T (GameQuest uses Valorant, Riot Games' tactical 5v5 shooter, as its theme. Player gamertags \(TenZ, zekken, Derke\), agents \(Jett, Raze, Sage, Sova, Phoenix\) and maps \(Ascent, Bind, Haven, Split, Icebox, Lotus\) are all taken from the game, so the data looks like something a real tournament would produce. The scoring is a simplified model written for this project and is not Riot's official ranking or rating formula.)
/E (GameQuest uses Valorant, Riot Games' tactical 5v5 shooter, as its theme. Player gamertags \(TenZ, zekken, Derke\), agents \(Jett, Raze, Sage, Sova, Phoenix\) and maps \(Ascent, Bind, Haven, Split, Icebox, Lotus\) are all taken from the game, so the data looks like something a real tournament would produce. The scoring is a simplified model written for this project and is not Riot's official ranking or rating formula.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
