>>
endobj
1518 0 obj
<<
/Title (schedules matches on Valorant maps, validates and records match statistics \(kills, deaths and assists\), ranks players on a live leaderboard, calculates rewards and saves the final results to text and CSV files. The project is deliberately built around six core object-oriented programming ideas: classes, inheritance, polymorphism, operator overloading, file handling and exception handling. Two player types, CasualPlayer and ProPlayer, share one virtual interface but score and get rewarded differently, which makes the polymorphism visible in the output. Invalid input such as negative or non-numeric scores is rejected by custom exceptions before it can corrupt the rankings.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1415 0 R
/Prev 1517 0 R
>>
endobj
1519 0 obj
<<
/Title (Esports tournaments generate a large amount of structured data: who is playing, what map they played on, how many kills they secured and where they finish in the table. Organisers usually track this in spreadsheets, which are easy to break and hard to validate. A small object-oriented program is a good way to model the same domain, because every real-world thing \(a player, a match, a tournament\) maps neatly to a class.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1416 0 R
/Next 1626 0 R
>>
endobj
